            "question": "Question 3(a): Study Sources B and C. How useful are Sources B and C for an enquiry into the collapse of the Kaiserreich and the proclamation of the German Republic in November 1918? Explain your answer, using Sources B and C and your knowledge of the historical context. [8 marks]",
            "examiner_tip": "Compare why BOTH announcements were rushed out on 9 November 1918: Prince Max acted from above to preserve constitutional order before Wilhelm II had formally signed his abdication, whereas Scheidemann spoke from the Reichstag window to stop radical Spartacists declaring a Soviet republic.",
            "model": "<p>Source B is highly useful for investigating the collapse of the Kaiserreich because it captures how imperial authorities tried to manage revolution from above. Printed on the front page of the official <em>Deutscher Reichsanzeiger</em> on 9 November 1918, it announces that \"The Kaiser and King has decided to renounce the throne\" and proposes \"the appointment of Deputy Ebert to the Imperial Chancellorship\" alongside \"the immediate summoning of a German National Assembly.\" Historically, following the Kiel Mutiny and general strikes in Berlin, Kaiser Wilhelm II at Spa still hesitated to abdicate. As an urgent official Chancellery proclamation issued unilaterally by Prince Max von Baden to calm rebellious crowds, its provenance is invaluable proof that moderate elites sacrificed the monarchy to prevent a violent Bolshevik-style upheaval.</p><p>Source C is equally useful because it reveals how popular street pressure forced moderate Social Democrats to proclaim a republic immediately. The press photograph shows SPD deputy Philipp Scheidemann leaning from a Reichstag window above hat-waving crowds, proclaiming \"militarism is finished! The Hohenzollerns have abdicated!\" and promising \"freedom and work.\" In reality, Scheidemann improvised this speech without Ebert's permission after learning that Spartacist leader Karl Liebknecht was preparing to declare a Soviet republic from the Berlin Palace two hours later. Directed publicly at \"Workers and soldiers!\" massed outside parliament, its provenance exposes the intense competition for street legitimacy on 9 November 1918.</p><p>Taken together, Sources B and C complement each other effectively, contrasting the improvised constitutional handover inside the Chancellery with the urgent popular politics outside the Reichstag.</p>",
