            "model": "<p>Interpretation 2 advances a highly convincing argument that economic catastrophe inflicted deeper, longer-lasting damage on the Weimar Republic than armed insurrections. Mary Fulbrook contends that Weimar \"was shaken far more profoundly by economic catastrophe than by right-wing putsches\", pointing to reparations, the January 1923 Ruhr occupation, and hyperinflation that \"completely destroyed the German currency.\" Contextual knowledge strongly corroborates this: saddled with £6.6 billion in reparations in May 1921, Germany defaulted on coal and timber deliveries, prompting 60,000 French and Belgian troops to occupy the industrial Ruhr. Chancellor Cuno's policy of printing unbacked paper Marks to pay striking workers during \"passive resistance\" pushed the exchange rate to 4.2 trillion Marks to the dollar by November 1923. As Fulbrook highlights, this \"wiped out the life savings, pensions, and financial security of the respectable middle class.\" Unlike street rebellions that collapsed within days, hyperinflation penalised thrifty <em>Mittelstand</em> savers, artisans, and pensioners across every German town, breeding \"a permanent legacy of bitterness and cynicism\" that later made middle-class voters receptive to nationalist extremism.</p><p>Nevertheless, Interpretation 1 provides an essential counter-perspective by demonstrating that without surviving repeated armed rebellions, the republic would have fallen long before 1923. Richard J. Evans argues that the \"gravest challenges\" were \"political violence, extremist insurrections, and the open disloyalty of the army.\" This is substantiated by relentless left- and right-wing bloodshed: the communist Spartacist Uprising of January 1919, the Red Ruhr Army revolt of 1920, and Hitler's Munich Putsch in November 1923. Crucially, Evans stresses that the March 1920 Kapp Putsch proved \"the regular military could not be relied upon to defend the constitution\" when General von Seeckt refused to fire on <em>Marinebrigade Ehrhardt</em>, leaving only a trade-union general strike to rescue Ebert's government. Furthermore, right-wing <em>Organisation Consul</em> gunmen carried out 376 political assassinations—including Matthias Erzberger (1921) and Foreign Minister Walther Rathenau (June 1922)—while conservative judges handed right-wing killers lenient sentences, exposing deep institutional rot.</p><p>Noticeably, the historians convey contrasting judgements through comparative and emphatic language: Fulbrook explicitly ranks threats (\"shaken far more profoundly... than by right-wing putsches\") to emphasise mass psychological alienation, whereas Evans uses urgent crisis imagery (\"assaulted\", \"violent lawlessness\", \"verge of collapse\") to foreground immediate physical overthrow. Overall, judged by breadth and permanence of impact, I largely agree with Interpretation 2: while paramilitary violence and judicial-military disloyalty posed acute existential emergencies between 1919 and 1922, every putsch failed, whereas the 1923 hyperinflation trauma permanently severed millions of ordinary middle-class citizens from loyalty to Weimar democracy.</p>",
