            "model": "<p>Source B is highly useful for investigating the genuine civic and social advances experienced by young urban women between 1924 and 1929. Writing in September 1927, Alice Rühle-Gerstel describes the \"New Woman\" with \"bobbed hair\" smoking in cafés, \"earning her own wages as a typist, shop assistant, or schoolmistress\", and exercising \"equal civil rights and... the vote\" under Article 109. Contextual knowledge confirms that Weimar granted female suffrage in 1918—achieving 90% turnout and 112 female Reichstag deputies across the period—while department stores and Americanised offices expanded female white-collar clerical employment fivefold compared with pre-war levels, alongside roughly 100,000 female teachers and 3,000 doctors. Published in <em>Die literarische Welt</em>, a progressive Berlin literary weekly, its metropolitan perspective makes it valuable eyewitness evidence of cosmopolitan middle-class aspirations, even if it overstates how typical Berlin café independence was outside major cities.</p><p>Source C is equally useful because it exposes both the structural economic limits of emancipation and the fierce conservative backlash provoked by Weimar modernity. Citing the 1925 Reich Occupational Census, the May 1928 DNVP Women's Committee statement notes that of 11.5 million working women, \"over four million toil without wages as family helpers on small farms\" and over one million work in domestic service, while factory women earn \"scarcely two-thirds of a man's wage\", before condemning \"shameless cabaret culture\" and \"falling birth rates\". Historically, women's overall employment share (36%) barely exceeded 1907 levels, women earned on average 33% less than men, married female civil servants were dismissed as <em>Doppelverdiener</em>, and Paragraph 218 kept abortion illegal as the birth rate fell from 25.9 (1920) to 18.6 per thousand (1928). Although partisan nationalist election propaganda designed to rally Christian voters against Weimar liberalism, its polemical tone is itself direct proof of how traditionalist hostility toward the \"New Woman\" polarised German society.</p>",
