                "level": "Level 2 Top / Level 3 Bottom (Two-Sided Contextual Account)",
                "text": "<p>On the one hand, Interpretation 2 is convincing that the \"New Woman\" was \"an urban myth\". Women were paid around 33% less than men, the 1900 Civil Code kept husbands in control of family finances, and married female civil servants were dismissed as double-earners. Millions worked unpaid on farms while groups like the DNVP attacked Berlin cabaret culture.</p><p>On the other hand, Interpretation 1 highlights real political and career progress. Article 109 gave women equal voting rights, leading to 90% turnout and nearly 10% of Reichstag deputies, alongside 100,000 female teachers and 3,000 doctors. Overall, I agree with Interpretation 2 because most rural and working-class women did not experience liberation.</p>",
