            "model": "<p>Source B is highly useful for investigating the early NSDAP because it sets out the ideological synthesis used to broaden a tiny Munich fringe group into a mass Bavarian movement. Proclaimed on 24 February 1920, the 25-Point Programme demands \"the unification of all Germans in the Greater Germany\", \"abrogation of the peace treaties of Versailles and St. Germain\", racial citizenship (\"No Jew, therefore, may be a member of the nation\"), and \"a strong central power in the Reich\". Historically, Hitler and Anton Drexler unveiled this programme before 2,000 attendees at the <em>Hofbräuhaus</em> as the DAP rebranded into the NSDAP, pairing <em>völkisch</em> nationalism with anti-capitalist pledges to attract both embittered ex-soldiers and workers. As a public founding manifesto designed for mass recruitment, its origin directly explains how party membership expanded from roughly 1,100 in mid-1920 to 55,000 by November 1923.</p><p>Source C is equally useful because it demonstrates the institutional and financial transformation of the party's propaganda machinery by late 1920. The front-page masthead of the <em>Völkischer Beobachter</em> dated 25 December 1920 in Munich—sold at \"50 Pf.\" and published twice weekly (\"Donnerstag und Sonntag\")—marks the NSDAP's acquisition of its own permanent newspaper. From contextual knowledge, nationalist mentor Dietrich Eckart and <em>Reichswehr</em> General Franz Ritter von Epp raised 180,000 marks (including secret army funds) to buy the paper, reaching a circulation of 17,000 within a year alongside Hitler's imposition of the <em>Führerprinzip</em> (July 1921) and the creation of Ernst Röhm's SA (August 1921). As an authentic Munich press artefact, it proves that sympathetic conservative patrons and organised media were vital to Hitler's early rise.</p><p>Taken together, Sources B and C show how populist doctrine and dedicated press infrastructure transformed the early NSDAP between 1920 and 1922.</p>",
