              "id": "weimar_l6_source_a",
              "letter": "C",
              "title": "Source C: Hitler's Closing Speech at the Munich Treason Trial (27 March 1924)",
              "type": "written",
              "text": "I consider myself neither a traitor nor a criminal. I wished for the best for my German people. The army that we have formed is growing from day to day... I have no desire to belong to a government that betrays its people. It is not you, gentlemen, who pass verdict upon us. That verdict will be spoken by the eternal court of history, which will judge the charges made against us. I know well your verdict, but that high court will not ask us: \"Did you commit high treason or did you not?\" That court will judge us, the Quartermaster-General of the old army [Ludendorff], his officers and soldiers, as Germans who wanted only the greatness of their fatherland. You may pronounce us guilty a thousand times over, but the goddess of the eternal court of history will smile and tear to tatters your verdict and the indictment of the state prosecutor; for she acquits us!",
              "caption": "Adolf Hitler's concluding address to the Munich People's Court on 27 March 1924 at the end of his trial for high treason following the Munich Beer Hall Putsch.",
