          "act_title": "Stage 2: Mass Unemployment & The \"Hunger Chancellor\"",
          "paragraphs": [
            "<span class=\"para-ref\">[2.1]</span> By winter 1932, German unemployment exploded into a devastating social catastrophe, leaving over **6 million unemployed** registered workers without income. Millions of desperate citizens queued for hours outside municipal labour exchanges or crowded soup kitchens, while homelessness soared across every major industrial city. When the democratic coalition government collapsed over unemployment insurance funding in March 1930, parliamentary democracy ceased to function effectively.",
            "<span class=\"para-ref\">[2.2]</span> President Hindenburg appointed Centre Party leader Heinrich Brüning, whose rigid **Heinrich Brüning deflation** policies worsened the crisis. Brüning enacted severe **Hunger Chancellor cuts** to wages, pensions, and unemployment relief, alienating ordinary working families. When the Reichstag rejected his austerity measures, Brüning dissolved parliament and governed through presidential emergency decrees under Article 48, fatally normalizing authoritarian rule without democratic parliamentary consent."
          ],
