            "model": "<p>Source B is highly useful for explaining why millions of voters abandoned moderate Weimar parties during the Great Depression. Enacted on 8 December 1931 \"On the basis of Article 48 of the Reich Constitution\", Chancellor Heinrich Brüning's Fourth Emergency Decree slashes \"civil service salaries, retirement pensions, and war disability allowances\" by \"nine percent\" and cuts \"unemployment insurance payments and crisis relief allowances\" by \"twelve percent\", restricting aid to \"complete personal indigence\". Following the October 1929 Wall Street Crash and recall of US Dawes/Young loans, unemployment reached six million by early 1932; governing by presidential decree (66 decrees in 1932 alone) earned Brüning the nickname \"Hunger Chancellor\" and alienated salaried <em>Mittelstand</em> families, war veterans, and workers alike. As an official statute in the <em>Reichsgesetzblatt</em>, its legal origin provides unimpeachable proof of both parliamentary paralysis and punitive deflation, though a statutory text cannot directly record voters' emotional reaction at the ballot box.</p><p>Source C is equally useful because it illustrates how the NSDAP projected discipline, strength, and national renewal amid that socio-economic crisis. The 1932 Berlin-Spandau press photograph shows a long, tightly ordered column of uniformed SA stormtroopers carrying a party banner down a cobbled street past trams, <em>Schutzpolizei</em> officers, and watching families. By 1932 Ernst Röhm's SA numbered 400,000 men; paired with Joseph Goebbels' modern electioneering (\"Hitler over Germany\" flights and \"Work and Bread\" posters), disciplined marches in working-class Berlin districts reassured anxious middle-class and rural voters that the Nazis could crush the rising KPD (which reached 89 seats in July 1932), propelling the NSDAP from 12 seats (1928) to 230 seats (37.3%) in July 1932. While a daytime campaign photograph omits the SA's brutal street violence and soup kitchens, its contemporary press origin captures the visual spectacle of order that drew voters to Hitler.</p>",
