            "model": "<p>Interpretation 2 makes a compelling case that economic misery alone did not automatically hand votes to the NSDAP; rather, active organisational and propagandistic skill enabled the Nazis to capitalise on the slump more effectively than any rival party. Richard J. Evans rightly observes that \"other nations suffered mass unemployment without turning to fascism\" and highlights \"brilliant modern electioneering and Hitler's magnetic personal appeal.\" Contextual knowledge strongly supports this: following the Bamberg Conference (1926) and the creation of 35 regional <em>Gaue</em>, Joseph Goebbels centralised propaganda to target tailored grievances—promising \"Work and Bread\" to the jobless, debt relief and tariffs to Protestant farmers in Schleswig-Holstein, and protection against Jewish department stores to <em>Mittelstand</em> shopkeepers. During the 1932 presidential race, Hitler's \"Hitler over Germany\" aircraft tour addressed twenty cities in seven days, winning 13.4 million votes. Simultaneously, as Evans notes, Ernst Röhm's 400,000 uniformed SA stormtroopers \"provided an intoxicating image of discipline and order on the streets\", running soup kitchens while battling the Communist <em>Rotfrontkämpferbund</em>, which convinced property-owning voters that only Hitler possessed the vigour to halt Bolshevism.</p><p>However, Interpretation 1 provides the indispensable structural explanation by demonstrating that without the Great Depression, Nazi propaganda fell on deaf ears. William Carr argues that the NSDAP became a mass movement \"primarily because the Great Depression shattered the economic and social foundations of the Weimar Republic.\" The decisive proof lies in the 1924–1928 \"Lean Years\": Hitler's oratory, the SA, and Goebbels' Berlin propaganda machine were already operating in May 1928, yet under Stresemann's economic stability the Nazis won a humiliating 2.6% of the vote (12 Reichstag seats). Only after the October 1929 Wall Street Crash triggered the recall of US loans, bankrupting five major banks and pushing unemployment to six million by early 1932, did Nazi support surge to 107 seats (1930) and 230 seats (37.3%, July 1932). Furthermore, as Carr stresses, the March 1930 collapse of Hermann Müller's Grand Coalition over unemployment benefits and Chancellor Brüning's punitive Article 48 wage and welfare cuts (\"Hunger Chancellor\") destroyed faith in parliamentary democracy while KPD support climbed to 89 seats, terrifying middle-class voters into Hitler's arms.</p><p>Noticeably, the historians convey contrasting causal priorities through language: Evans uses comparative reasoning (\"cannot be explained solely\") and vivid sensory adjectives (\"magnetic\", \"dynamic\", \"intoxicating\") to foreground Nazi agency, whereas Carr employs structural language (\"shattered the economic and social foundations\", \"economic paralysis\") to emphasise systemic breakdown. Overall, distinguishing underlying precondition from active catalyst, I agree with Interpretation 2 only partially: Goebbels' propaganda and the SA determined <em>which</em> radical party harvested protest votes, but as 1928 proved, the Great Depression in Interpretation 1 was the decisive precondition that made mass Nazi growth possible.</p>",
