            "question": "Question 3(a): Study Sources B and C. How useful are Sources B and C for an enquiry into how Hitler dismantled democracy and established a one-party dictatorship in 1933? Explain your answer, using Sources B and C and your knowledge of the historical context. [8 marks]",
            "examiner_tip": "Trace the legal escalation across the four months between Sources B and C: Wels' defiant speech in the Kroll Opera House (23 March 1933) highlights the coercion and Catholic Centre capitulation behind the Enabling Act, while the 14 July 1933 statute used those decree powers to outlaw all rival parties.",
            "model": "<p>Source B is highly useful for investigating the destruction of parliamentary democracy during the passage of the Enabling Act on 23 March 1933. Addressing the Reichstag inside Berlin's Kroll Opera House, SPD Chairman Otto Wels warns Hitler that \"No Enabling Act gives you the power to extinguish ideas that are eternal and indestructible\" and concludes \"Freedom and life can be taken from us, but our honour, never!\" Historically, after winning 43.9% in the 5 March election, Hitler still needed a two-thirds constitutional majority; he barred 81 Communist deputies under the Reichstag Fire Decree, stationed chanting SA and SS guards along the aisles, and won over the Catholic Centre Party with false assurances over church schools, passing the Act by 444 votes to 94. Preserved in the official Reichstag stenographic record as the sole speech opposing four-year rule by cabinet decree, its courageous yet isolated tone underscores both Nazi physical intimidation and the fatal capitulation of bourgeois parties.</p><p>Source C is equally useful because it documents the legal completion of the one-party state less than four months later. Enacted on 14 July 1933, the Law Against the Establishment of Parties decrees in Article 1 that \"The National Socialist German Workers’ Party constitutes the only political party in Germany\" and punishes any attempt to maintain or form rival parties with \"penal servitude up to three years\". Between March and July 1933, Hitler used Enabling Act decrees to coordinate (<em>Gleichschaltung</em>) state governments, smash trade unions on 2 May, ban the SPD in June, and pressure conservative and centrist parties into self-dissolution. As binding statutory law published in the <em>Reichsgesetzblatt</em>, it provides definitive constitutional proof of how Hitler used pseudo-legal decrees to criminalise all political opposition.</p><p>Taken together, Sources B and C chart the decisive transition from parliamentary capitulation in March 1933 to codified totalitarian monopoly by July 1933.</p>",
