            "question": "Question 3(a): Study Sources B and C. How useful are Sources B and C for an enquiry into the methods used by the Nazi police state to control German society in the years 1933–1939? Explain your answer, using Sources B and C and your knowledge of the historical context. [8 marks]",
            "examiner_tip": "Avoid claiming the Gestapo had an agent on every street corner: use Source B to prove how a tiny secret-police force relied on neighbourly denunciations and camp internment (Schutzhaft), and use Source C to show how the People's Court destroyed judicial independence.",
            "model": "<p>Source B is highly useful for investigating the Nazi police state because it exposes how secret-police terror depended on grassroots civilian complicity. Compiled on 14 May 1936 in Düsseldorf, the dossier records an anonymous denunciation by neighbour \"Frau B.\" against grocer \"Citizen Friedrich K.\" for failing to hang a swastika flag on Hitler's birthday and remarking that \"The brown bigwigs live off butter and meat while we must make sacrifices for the four-year plan\", resulting in indefinite \"protective custody (Schutzhaft)\" at \"Esterwegen concentration camp\". Historically, Himmler and Heydrich's Gestapo numbered under 30,000 officers nationwide (fewer than 30 in cities like Frankfurt), meaning upwards of 70–80% of political investigations originated from voluntary civilian tip-offs and <em>Blockwarts</em>, backed by extrajudicial SS camps opened from Dachau (1933) onwards. As a confidential internal police protocol written for administrative record-keeping rather than public propaganda, it offers candid proof of how petty denunciations and arbitrary internment enforced everyday conformity.</p><p>Source C is equally useful because it demonstrates how the regime systematically subordinated the formal justice system to Nazi ideology. Enacted on 24 April 1934, the statute establishes the People's Court (<em>Volksgerichtshof</em>) for treason cases, staffing benches with Chancellor-appointed judges alongside \"honorary members selected from the Schutzstaffel (SS), the SA, and the National Socialist German Workers' Party\", operating \"without a jury\", restricting defence lawyers to an approved list, and allowing \"no appeal\". Created after Hitler's fury when the Supreme Court acquitted three defendants in the 1933 Reichstag Fire trial—and reinforced by compulsory judicial membership in the National Socialist League for the Maintenance of the Law—this official <em>Reichsgesetzblatt</em> statute proves that legal due process was abolished from above.</p><p>Combined, Sources B and C show how extrajudicial Gestapo surveillance and politicised courts worked in tandem to eliminate legal protection for German citizens.</p>",
