            "model": "<p>Source B is highly useful for investigating how the Nazi regime used modern consumer technology to carry state persuasion into private homes. The 1933 artefact—a brown Bakelite <em>Volksempfänger</em> VE301 bearing an eagle emblem, circular speaker grille, and restricted tuning dial—was subsidised by Joseph Goebbels' Propaganda Ministry at 76 Reichsmarks (followed by the 35-RM <em>Deutscher Kleinempfänger</em>). Contextual knowledge confirms that household radio ownership surged from 25% to 70% by 1939, supplemented by 6,000 public loudspeaker pillars, blending popular music with Nuremberg Rally relays to cultivate the \"Hitler Myth\" while technically blocking foreign broadcasts like the BBC. As an authentic state-engineered consumer product, its physical design proves Goebbels combined mass entertainment with built-in censorship, though an object alone cannot reveal whether listeners genuinely believed every broadcast.</p><p>Source C is equally useful because it exposes the coercive surveillance and terror that enforced outward conformity whenever propaganda failed to convince. In the May 1936 Düsseldorf Gestapo dossier, grocer Friedrich K. is denounced anonymously by his neighbour Frau B. for failing to fly a swastika banner on Hitler's birthday and complaining that \"brown bigwigs live off butter and meat\", leading directly to \"protective custody (<em>Schutzhaft</em>)\" in Esterwegen concentration camp. Historically, although the Gestapo employed fewer than 30,000 officers nationwide, the 1934 Malicious Gossip Act, Nazi <em>Blockwarte</em> (block wardens), and voluntary civilian denunciations created an atmosphere of pervasive fear backed by SS camps like Dachau. Preserved as a confidential internal police record never meant for public propaganda, its bureaucratic candour provides unimpeachable evidence that fear of arrest silenced everyday grumbling.</p>",
