            "model": "<p>Interpretation 2 presents a powerful argument that beneath the choreographed surface of Nazi rallies, coercive terror was indispensable in policing public attitudes. Richard J. Evans contends that national unity was \"fundamentally an illusion created by the omnipresence of the terror state\", enforced by \"denunciation to the Gestapo, the pervasive network of SS surveillance, and the dread of protective custody in concentration camps like Dachau.\" Contextual knowledge strongly substantiates this machinery of repression. Following the February 1933 Reichstag Fire Decree, Heinrich Himmler's SS established Dachau (March 1933), Sachsenhausen, and Buchenwald to hold political prisoners indefinitely under <em>Schutzhaft</em> without trial. Although the Gestapo numbered fewer than 30,000 officers, local Nazi <em>Blockwarte</em> (block wardens) and voluntary denunciations under the 1934 Malicious Gossip Act meant anti-regime jokes risked prison, while Roland Freisler's People's Court (<em>Volksgerichtshof</em>) tried treason without appeal. Moreover, Goebbels' own propaganda relied on coercion: the October 1933 Editors' Law closed 1,600 newspapers, the May 1933 book burnings purged 20,000 works, and clandestine <em>Sopade</em> reports confirm working-class cynicism toward Nazi Party bosses.</p><p>However, Interpretation 1 offers a vital corrective by showing that terror alone cannot explain the immense popular acclaim Hitler enjoyed between 1933 and 1939, nor why millions willingly denounced their neighbours to the Gestapo. David Welch argues that propaganda succeeded because it appealed \"to existing national traditions and pride\", fostering \"a genuine sense of national community (<em>Volksgemeinschaft</em>)\" and \"genuine enthusiasm and admiration for Hitler.\" Historically, Goebbels' Reich Chamber of Culture skillfully blended entertainment with ideology: 70% of households bought the 76-RM <em>Volksempfänger</em>, cinema attendance doubled to over one billion tickets by 1939 alongside Leni Riefenstahl's <em>Triumph of the Will</em>, Albert Speer's 152-searchlight \"Cathedral of Light\" electrified hundreds of thousands at Nuremberg, and the 1936 Berlin Olympics showcased national prestige. When plebiscites endorsed leaving the League of Nations or remilitarising the Rhineland (1936), the \"Hitler Myth\"—crediting Hitler personally for ending Depression unemployment and tearing up Versailles—commanded sincere cross-class popularity.</p><p>Crucially, the two historians convey contrasting verdicts through tone: Evans employs sceptical, unmasking language (\"apparent unity\", \"illusion\", \"loud and relentless\") to strip away regime staging, whereas Welch uses empathetic cultural vocabulary (\"hearts and minds\", \"euphoric pageantry\", \"genuine enthusiasm\") to explain popular consent. Overall, judged across the whole population, I agree with Interpretation 2 only partially: propaganda and terror functioned as mutually reinforcing halves of one system—propaganda generated genuine nationalist enthusiasm around the \"Hitler Myth\" for the conformist majority, while Gestapo terror crushed dissenters and deterred sceptics from ever breaking ranks.</p>",
