                "title": "8/16 Two-Sided Account",
                "score": "8 / 16 Marks",
                "level": "Level 2 Top / Level 3 Bottom (Two-Sided Contextual Account)",
                "text": "<p>On the one hand, Interpretation 2 is convincing that opposition was \"extremely limited, narrow\". The Catholic Church signed the Concordat in July 1933 and shut down the Centre Party, most Protestant pastors took an oath to Hitler in 1938, and the Churches stayed silent during the 1935 Nuremberg Laws and Kristallnacht in 1938.</p><p>On the other hand, Interpretation 1 shows brave resistance to Nazification. Martin Niemöller and Dietrich Bonhoeffer formed the Confessing Church with 6,000 pastors against Reich Bishop Müller, Pope Pius XI issued Mit brennender Sorge in 1937, and 800 pastors plus 400 Catholic priests were sent to prisons and Dachau. Overall, I agree with Interpretation 2 because pastors defended church rules rather than trying to overthrow Hitler.</p>",
                "verdict": "AO4 Level 2/3 (8 marks) • SPaG Intermediate (2 marks). Supports both views with accurate contextual examples and states a clear conclusion, though analysis of authorial tone/criteria remains undeveloped."
