            "examiner_tip": "Check the dates of Sources B (1933) and C (1935) against the whole 1933–1939 period: both reveal early pro-natalist incentives and Kinder, Küche, Kirche ideology, but own contextual knowledge must note how rearmament labour shortages after 1937 forced the regime to pull women back into factories.",
            "model": "<p>Source B is highly useful for investigating Nazi family policy because it reveals how financial incentives merged pro-natalism, racial selection, and labour-market manipulation. Enacted on 1 June 1933, the Law for the Encouragement of Marriage offers \"German citizens of Aryan descent\" an interest-free \"marriage loan of up to 1,000 Reichsmarks\" in household vouchers, strictly conditional on \"the female partner renouncing her permanent employment\", while remitting \"twenty-five percent\" for each living child. Historically, paired with the dismissal of female judges, doctors, and civil servants, over 800,000 couples took marriage loans and annual births rose from 970,000 in 1933 to 1.41 million by 1939. As binding early legislation in the <em>Reichsgesetzblatt</em>, it proves how law pushed women out of jobs, though its June 1933 date misses the major policy reversal of 1937, when rearmament labour shortages forced the regime to scrap the employment ban and female workers rose to 14.6 million by 1939.</p><p>Source C is equally useful because it articulates the racial-maternal ideology used to justify women's exclusion from public life. Addressing the Nazi Women's League at the September 1935 Nuremberg Rally, Reich Women's Leader Gertrud Scholtz-Klink proclaims \"our battlefield is the nursery and the family hearth\", framing it as a \"holy duty to bear healthy, pure-blooded children for the Führer\" and rejecting \"superficial emancipation in parliament or commerce\". This doctrine underpinned the six-million-strong <em>Deutsches Frauenwerk</em>, the Gold Mother's Cross for eight children, and Himmler's 1935 <em>Lebensborn</em> homes. Delivered publicly at a showcase party congress by the regime's top female official, its propagandistic purpose cannot prove ordinary women privately embraced <em>Kinder, Küche, Kirche</em>, yet it authoritatively exposes official gender orthodoxy.</p><p>Together, Sources B and C capture the statutory incentives and ideological indoctrination driving Nazi policy toward women, which contextual knowledge shows later collided with war-economy demands.</p>",
