      "prologue": "Between 1933 and 1939, the Nazi regime claimed to have achieved an astonishing economic miracle: reducing unemployment from six million to virtually zero. Yet behind this triumph lay brutal coercion and statistical manipulation. Independent trade unions were smashed on 2 May 1933 and replaced by Robert Ley's compulsory German Labour Front (DAF). Unemployed young men were conscripted into the Reich Labour Service (RAD) to dig autobahns with spades, while Jews, women, and political opponents were erased from unemployment registers. To placate workers who endured eighty-hour weeks and frozen wages, the regime offered subsidised theatre tickets, cruises, and the promise of a Volkswagen Beetle through the Strength Through Joy (KdF) movement. Did Nazi economic policies genuinely improve the standard of living for German workers, or merely disguise coercion and militarisation?",
      "specification_anchor": "Life in Nazi Germany, 1933–39: employment and living standards; policies to reduce unemployment (RAD, autobahns, rearmament, invisible unemployment); standard of living, the DAF, KdF and Beauty of Labour.",
      "hook_text": "Between 1933 and 1939, the Nazi regime claimed to have achieved an astonishing economic miracle: reducing unemployment from six million to virtually zero. Yet behind this triumph lay brutal coercion and statistical manipulation. Independent trade unions were smashed on 2 May 1933 and replaced by Robert Ley's compulsory German Labour Front (DAF). Unemployed young men were conscripted into the Reich Labour Service (RAD) to dig autobahns with spades, while Jews, women, and political opponents were erased from unemployment registers. To placate workers who endured eighty-hour weeks and frozen wages, the regime offered subsidised theatre tickets, cruises, and the promise of a Volkswagen Beetle through the Strength Through Joy (KdF) movement. Did Nazi economic policies genuinely improve the standard of living for German workers, or merely disguise coercion and militarisation?",
